WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.736 --> 00:11.106 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference June 19,

00:11.136 --> 00:12.656 align:middle
2019 CHAIR POWELL.

00:12.656 --> 00:13.946 align:middle
Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:13.946 --> 00:16.466 align:middle
My colleagues and I have
one overarching goal:

00:17.056 --> 00:18.796 align:middle
to sustain the economic
expansion,

00:19.386 --> 00:22.196 align:middle
with a strong job market and
stable prices, for the benefit

00:22.196 --> 00:23.106 align:middle
of the American people.

00:23.786 --> 00:27.006 align:middle
At the FOMC meeting
that concluded today,

00:27.006 --> 00:28.886 align:middle
we maintained our
policy interest rate

00:29.146 --> 00:31.806 align:middle
but made some significant
changes to our statement.

00:33.016 --> 00:34.756 align:middle
Since the beginning of
the year, we have judged

00:34.756 --> 00:37.546 align:middle
that our current policy
stance was broadly appropriate

00:37.736 --> 00:38.876 align:middle
and that we should be patient

00:39.006 --> 00:40.646 align:middle
in assessing the
need for any changes.

00:41.826 --> 00:43.406 align:middle
In light of increased
uncertainties

00:43.406 --> 00:46.226 align:middle
and muted inflation
pressures, we now emphasize

00:46.226 --> 00:48.606 align:middle
that the Committee will
closely monitor the implications

00:48.606 --> 00:52.086 align:middle
of incoming information for the
economic outlook and will act

00:52.086 --> 00:53.986 align:middle
as appropriate to
sustain the expansion

00:54.436 --> 00:55.576 align:middle
with a strong labor market

00:55.606 --> 00:57.796 align:middle
and inflation near its
2 percent objective.

00:59.406 --> 01:02.476 align:middle
I'd like to step back and
review how the changing economic

01:02.476 --> 01:05.216 align:middle
and financial picture brings
us to today's decision.

01:06.616 --> 01:09.906 align:middle
So far this year, the economy
has performed reasonably well,

01:10.116 --> 01:12.536 align:middle
with solid fundamentals
supporting continued growth

01:12.536 --> 01:13.636 align:middle
and strong employment.

01:14.966 --> 01:17.386 align:middle
Inflation has been running
somewhat below our objective,

01:17.556 --> 01:20.646 align:middle
but we have expected it to pick
up, supported by solid growth

01:20.646 --> 01:21.856 align:middle
and a strong job market.

01:22.936 --> 01:25.256 align:middle
Along with this favorable
picture, we have been mindful

01:25.256 --> 01:29.166 align:middle
of some ongoing crosscurrents,
including trade developments

01:29.246 --> 01:30.896 align:middle
and concerns about
global growth.

01:32.346 --> 01:35.456 align:middle
At the time of our last FOMC
meeting, which ended on May 1,

01:35.596 --> 01:36.846 align:middle
there was tentative evidence

01:36.896 --> 01:38.586 align:middle
that these crosscurrents
were moderating.

01:39.616 --> 01:42.006 align:middle
The latest data from China
and Europe were encouraging,

01:42.666 --> 01:44.376 align:middle
and there were reports
of progress

01:44.376 --> 01:45.896 align:middle
in trade negotiations
with China.

01:45.896 --> 01:49.186 align:middle
Our continued patient
stance seemed appropriate,

01:49.796 --> 01:51.996 align:middle
and the Committee
saw no strong case

01:52.206 --> 01:53.646 align:middle
for adjusting our policy rate.

01:55.416 --> 01:56.756 align:middle
In the weeks since
our last meeting,

01:56.756 --> 01:58.336 align:middle
the crosscurrents
have reemerged.

01:58.876 --> 02:01.406 align:middle
Growth indicators from around
the world have disappointed,

02:01.406 --> 02:03.886 align:middle
on net, raising concerns
about the strength

02:03.886 --> 02:04.796 align:middle
of the global economy.

02:06.136 --> 02:08.906 align:middle
Apparent progress on trade
turned to greater uncertainty,

02:09.576 --> 02:11.136 align:middle
and our contacts in business

02:11.136 --> 02:13.186 align:middle
and agriculture report
heightened concerns

02:13.186 --> 02:14.256 align:middle
over trade developments.

02:15.856 --> 02:17.896 align:middle
These concerns may have
contributed to the drop

02:17.896 --> 02:20.136 align:middle
in business confidence
in some recent surveys

02:20.716 --> 02:23.116 align:middle
and may be starting to show
through to incoming data.

02:24.536 --> 02:26.786 align:middle
Risk sentiment in financial
markets has deteriorated

02:26.786 --> 02:27.266 align:middle
as well.

02:27.656 --> 02:30.246 align:middle
Against this backdrop,
inflation remains muted.

02:31.876 --> 02:34.166 align:middle
While the baseline
outlook remains favorable,

02:34.236 --> 02:36.766 align:middle
the question is whether these
uncertainties will continue

02:36.766 --> 02:38.566 align:middle
to weigh on the outlook
and thus call

02:38.566 --> 02:40.946 align:middle
for additional monetary
policy accommodation.

02:42.376 --> 02:45.336 align:middle
Many FOMC participants
now see that the case

02:45.336 --> 02:47.296 align:middle
for a somewhat more
accommodative policy

02:47.296 --> 02:48.016 align:middle
has strengthened.

02:49.346 --> 02:51.386 align:middle
Let me explain the
basis for this judgment,

02:51.386 --> 02:53.536 align:middle
starting with the outlook
for jobs and growth.

02:55.546 --> 02:58.276 align:middle
Participants see unemployment
remaining low this year

02:58.276 --> 02:58.826 align:middle
and next.

02:59.836 --> 03:02.946 align:middle
Monthly job gains in May were
lower than expected, however,

03:03.036 --> 03:04.396 align:middle
and, in light of
recent developments,

03:04.466 --> 03:05.616 align:middle
this bears watching.

03:06.286 --> 03:08.866 align:middle
Still, many labor market
indicators remain strong.

03:09.736 --> 03:12.066 align:middle
Community, business, and
labor leaders all tell us

03:12.116 --> 03:14.766 align:middle
that the prospects for job
seekers have seldom been better,

03:15.266 --> 03:16.786 align:middle
and that this is
true even for those

03:16.786 --> 03:18.606 align:middle
who have traditionally
struggled to find work.

03:19.856 --> 03:21.786 align:middle
Wages are rising, and
this is particularly

03:21.786 --> 03:23.486 align:middle
so for lower-paying jobs.

03:25.926 --> 03:27.656 align:middle
Committee participants'
growth projections

03:27.656 --> 03:30.266 align:middle
from 2019 are little
revised from March,

03:30.346 --> 03:32.936 align:middle
with a central tendency of
2 percent to 2.2 percent,

03:33.486 --> 03:36.586 align:middle
just above their estimates
of longer-run normal growth.

03:37.736 --> 03:39.296 align:middle
The growth projections
for the year

03:39.296 --> 03:41.526 align:middle
as a whole mask some
important details

03:41.526 --> 03:43.006 align:middle
about the composition of growth.

03:44.486 --> 03:45.796 align:middle
Annual growth will be boosted

03:45.856 --> 03:47.876 align:middle
by the surprisingly
strong first quarter,

03:48.256 --> 03:50.086 align:middle
which had just been
reported at the time

03:50.086 --> 03:51.406 align:middle
of the May FOMC meeting.

03:52.576 --> 03:55.566 align:middle
As I noted then, the
unexpected strength was largely

03:55.566 --> 03:58.186 align:middle
in net exports and
inventories-components

03:58.226 --> 04:01.526 align:middle
that are not generally reliable
indicators of ongoing momentum.

04:02.246 --> 04:04.876 align:middle
The more reliable
drivers of growth

04:04.876 --> 04:06.856 align:middle
in the economy are
spending on consumption

04:06.856 --> 04:07.826 align:middle
and business investment.

04:08.926 --> 04:10.856 align:middle
While consumption was
weak in the first quarter,

04:11.116 --> 04:13.806 align:middle
incoming data show that
it has bounced back

04:13.996 --> 04:15.836 align:middle
and is now running
at a solid pace.

04:17.086 --> 04:19.236 align:middle
In contrast, the limited
evidence available

04:19.236 --> 04:20.976 align:middle
at this time suggests
that growth

04:20.976 --> 04:23.026 align:middle
in business fixed
income has slowed

04:23.026 --> 04:25.586 align:middle
in the second quarter.economy
are spending on consumption

04:25.586 --> 04:26.336 align:middle
and business investment.

04:26.336 --> 04:27.496 align:middle
While consumption was
weak in the first quarter,

04:27.496 --> 04:28.926 align:middle
incoming data show that
it has bounced back

04:28.926 --> 04:30.186 align:middle
and is now running
at a solid pace.

04:30.186 --> 04:31.776 align:middle
In contrast, the limited
evidence available

04:31.776 --> 04:32.776 align:middle
