04:43.476 --> 04:44.456 align:middle
1 Chair Powell intended
to say that growth

04:44.546 --> 04:46.046 align:middle
in business fixed investment
slowed in the second quarter.

04:46.046 --> 04:48.576 align:middle
After running close to our
symmetric 2 percent objective

04:48.646 --> 04:52.036 align:middle
for most of last year, inflation
declined in the first quarter.

04:53.076 --> 04:54.836 align:middle
Data since then show
some pickup.

04:55.846 --> 04:58.436 align:middle
Participants broadly see
inflation moving back

04:58.436 --> 05:01.496 align:middle
up toward our 2 percent
objective, but at a slower pace

05:01.556 --> 05:02.546 align:middle
than had been expected.

05:03.526 --> 05:06.426 align:middle
The central tendency
for 2019 core inflation,

05:06.686 --> 05:09.156 align:middle
which omits volatile food
and energy components,

05:10.196 --> 05:12.586 align:middle
is between 1.7 and 1.8 percent.

05:13.906 --> 05:15.906 align:middle
Setting aside short-term
fluctuations,

05:15.906 --> 05:18.626 align:middle
Committee participants expressed
concerns about the pace

05:18.626 --> 05:20.406 align:middle
of inflation's return
to 2 percent.

05:21.626 --> 05:24.276 align:middle
Wages are rising, as noted
above, but not at a pace

05:24.276 --> 05:26.696 align:middle
that would provide much
upward impetus to inflation.

05:27.486 --> 05:31.086 align:middle
Moreover, weaker global growth
may continue to hold inflation

05:31.086 --> 05:32.106 align:middle
down around the world.

05:33.476 --> 05:34.516 align:middle
We are firmly committed

05:34.516 --> 05:36.956 align:middle
to our symmetric 2 percent
inflation objective,

05:36.956 --> 05:39.336 align:middle
and we are well aware
that inflation weakness

05:39.376 --> 05:42.376 align:middle
that persists even in a healthy
economy could precipitate a

05:42.746 --> 05:44.876 align:middle
difficult-to-arrest
downward drift

05:44.986 --> 05:46.956 align:middle
in longer-run inflation
expectations.

05:48.086 --> 05:49.626 align:middle
Because there are no
definitive measures

05:49.626 --> 05:52.846 align:middle
of inflation expectations, we
must rely on imperfect proxies.

05:53.666 --> 05:54.606 align:middle
Market-based measures

05:54.606 --> 05:56.206 align:middle
of inflation compensation
have moved

05:56.206 --> 05:57.426 align:middle
down since our May meeting,

05:57.916 --> 06:00.646 align:middle
and some survey-based
expectations measures are near

06:00.646 --> 06:02.236 align:middle
the bottom of their
historic ranges.

06:03.126 --> 06:06.126 align:middle
Combining these factors with the
risks to growth already noted,

06:06.346 --> 06:08.116 align:middle
participants expressed concerns

06:08.116 --> 06:11.006 align:middle
about a more sustained
shortfall of inflation.

06:12.876 --> 06:15.006 align:middle
Overall, our policy
discussions focused

06:15.006 --> 06:17.646 align:middle
on the appropriate response
to the uncertain environment.

06:18.696 --> 06:20.496 align:middle
The projections of
appropriate policy show

06:20.496 --> 06:22.696 align:middle
that many participants
believe that some cut

06:22.696 --> 06:25.106 align:middle
in the federal funds rate will
be appropriate in the scenario

06:25.106 --> 06:26.486 align:middle
that they see as most likely.

06:27.676 --> 06:29.936 align:middle
Though some participants
wrote down policy cuts

06:29.936 --> 06:32.736 align:middle
and others did not, our
deliberations made clear

06:32.736 --> 06:35.706 align:middle
that a number of those who wrote
down a flat rate path agree

06:35.706 --> 06:38.266 align:middle
that the case for additional
accommodation has strengthened

06:38.756 --> 06:39.676 align:middle
since our May meeting.

06:40.876 --> 06:44.086 align:middle
This added accommodation would
support economic activity

06:44.086 --> 06:46.036 align:middle
and inflation's return
to our objective.

06:47.256 --> 06:49.926 align:middle
Uncertainties surrounding the
baseline outlook have clearly

06:49.926 --> 06:51.256 align:middle
risen since our last meeting.

06:51.616 --> 06:54.946 align:middle
It is important, however, that
monetary policy not overreact

06:54.946 --> 06:58.666 align:middle
to any individual data point or
short-term swing in sentiment.

06:59.706 --> 07:03.536 align:middle
Doing so would risk adding even
more uncertainty to the outlook.

07:04.206 --> 07:05.906 align:middle
Thus, my colleagues
and I will be looking

07:05.906 --> 07:07.876 align:middle
to see whether these
uncertainties will continue

07:07.876 --> 07:10.826 align:middle
to weigh on the outlook,
and we will use our tools

07:10.826 --> 07:13.276 align:middle
as appropriate to
sustain the expansion.

07:14.136 --> 07:14.556 align:middle
Thank you.

07:14.556 --> 07:17.316 align:middle
I will be pleased to
take your questions.

07:17.466 --> 07:20.576 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

07:21.436 --> 07:23.786 align:middle
Nick Timiraos, Wall
Street Journal.

07:24.246 --> 07:26.986 align:middle
Chair Powell, did you
consider a rate cut today?

07:27.036 --> 07:28.436 align:middle
Specifically, was it one

07:28.436 --> 07:31.606 align:middle
of the options-the policy
options in the Tealbook?

07:32.006 --> 07:34.066 align:middle
And is the Committee
considering moving,

07:34.066 --> 07:36.826 align:middle
given all the uncertainty
you addressed,

07:36.876 --> 07:39.956 align:middle
moving its-changing its policy
before the next meeting?

07:40.466 --> 07:42.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

07:43.336 --> 07:49.046 align:middle
So the Committee had, you
know, our usual long discussion

07:49.046 --> 07:52.536 align:middle
of global and domestic economic
and financial conditions

07:53.216 --> 07:56.976 align:middle
and then spent this morning
talking about monetary policy.

07:57.656 --> 08:01.436 align:middle
And I came to the view
that I expressed to you,

08:01.576 --> 08:04.406 align:middle
which is that we're going

08:04.616 --> 08:08.436 align:middle
to be monitoring-monitoring
the crosscurrents

08:08.656 --> 08:11.166 align:middle
and the other items that
we've mentioned, but that we'd

08:11.166 --> 08:14.386 align:middle
like to see more going forward.

08:14.386 --> 08:17.266 align:middle
Particularly, we'd like to see
whether these risks continue

08:17.266 --> 08:18.046 align:middle
to weigh on the outlook.

08:18.896 --> 08:22.296 align:middle
So, generally, as I mentioned,

08:23.016 --> 08:25.836 align:middle
many on the Committee do see
a strengthened case-eight

08:25.836 --> 08:28.676 align:middle
of those, a strengthened
case for cutting rates.

08:28.836 --> 08:30.356 align:middle
Eight actually wrote
down rate cuts.

08:30.396 --> 08:33.496 align:middle
A number of others see that
the case has strengthened.

08:34.106 --> 08:38.156 align:middle
But the Committee wanted to
see more, as I mentioned.

08:38.156 --> 08:41.036 align:middle
And I also mentioned
that some of these-some

08:41.916 --> 08:44.886 align:middle
of these developments have
been of quite recent vintage.

08:45.066 --> 08:50.226 align:middle
And so we do expect that we'll
be learning a lot more on all

08:50.226 --> 08:51.706 align:middle
of these issues in
the near term.

08:51.976 --> 08:54.056 align:middle
And that's our focus.

08:54.866 --> 08:55.236 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

08:55.236 --> 08:57.576 align:middle
Do you think something could
change before the next meeting?

08:57.576 --> 08:58.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:58.856 --> 09:00.716 align:middle
I'm sure that things will
change before the next meeting.

09:00.716 --> 09:03.336 align:middle
I expect a full range of
data and information on all

09:03.336 --> 09:05.646 align:middle
of these issues that
we are looking at.

09:05.856 --> 09:09.686 align:middle
I think we'll learn a
great deal more about them.

09:09.686 --> 09:11.506 align:middle
And I think that's-we think

09:11.506 --> 09:13.746 align:middle
that that's the right
way to move here.

09:14.116 --> 09:16.846 align:middle
Again, many of these
developments happened, you know,

09:17.186 --> 09:19.406 align:middle
part of the way through the
last intermeeting period.

09:19.486 --> 09:23.466 align:middle
Only seven weeks ago, we had
a great jobs report and came

09:23.466 --> 09:26.316 align:middle
out of the last FOMC meeting
feeling that the economy,

09:26.316 --> 09:27.636 align:middle
and our policy, was
in a good place.

09:27.636 --> 09:31.226 align:middle
So we want to see-we want
to see and we want to react

09:31.226 --> 09:36.666 align:middle
to developments and trends that
are sustained, that are genuine,

09:36.736 --> 09:40.426 align:middle
and not react just to data
points or just to changes

09:40.426 --> 09:43.376 align:middle
in sentiment, which
can be volatile.

09:43.376 --> 09:47.546 align:middle
At the same time, we are-we're
quite mindful of the risks

09:47.546 --> 09:50.796 align:middle
to the outlook and are prepared
to move and use our tools

09:50.796 --> 09:52.416 align:middle
as needed to sustain
the expansion.

09:54.366 --> 09:55.386 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

09:57.296 --> 09:59.506 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Steve
Liesman, CNBC.

09:59.666 --> 10:02.416 align:middle
Could you walk us through
your thinking about trade?

10:02.416 --> 10:06.346 align:middle
It was really the threat
of tariffs against Mexico

10:06.346 --> 10:11.066 align:middle
that caused at least the market
to become definitively banking,

10:11.066 --> 10:13.506 align:middle
or-pricing in rate cuts.

10:14.026 --> 10:16.656 align:middle
If, for example, there's
a deal with China,

10:17.446 --> 10:20.366 align:middle
does that take the possibility
of rate cuts off the table?

10:21.316 --> 10:22.016 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:22.016 --> 10:27.936 align:middle
Yeah, so I would say that we're
not looking at any one thing.

10:28.086 --> 10:30.556 align:middle
I guess I would start by
agreeing with your premise

10:31.376 --> 10:35.706 align:middle
that news about trade has been
an important driver of sentiment

10:35.826 --> 10:37.296 align:middle
in the intermeeting period.

10:37.926 --> 10:40.816 align:middle
But we're also looking
at global growth.

10:40.816 --> 10:44.066 align:middle
It's really trade developments
and concerns about global growth

10:44.066 --> 10:46.176 align:middle
that are on our minds.

10:46.486 --> 10:47.956 align:middle
So we're not exclusively focused

10:47.956 --> 10:49.656 align:middle
on one event or one
piece of data.

10:50.176 --> 10:51.666 align:middle
Risks seem to have grown.

10:51.666 --> 10:55.196 align:middle
In the meantime, we
have incoming data

10:55.196 --> 10:57.386 align:middle
in the United States that's
been pretty good, particularly

10:57.386 --> 11:00.966 align:middle
for the consumer: Consumer
spending is solid supported by,

11:00.966 --> 11:03.176 align:middle
you know, a healthy job market,

11:03.376 --> 11:05.396 align:middle
high levels of employment,
wages going up.

11:06.116 --> 11:09.096 align:middle
We do see, though, some areas
that we're looking at, such as,

11:09.186 --> 11:13.046 align:middle
I mentioned, business
fixed income.2 So-also,

11:13.046 --> 11:15.176 align:middle
the prolonged shortfall
in inflation

11:15.176 --> 11:19.196 align:middle
and perhaps job growth-we don't
like to look at one job report.

11:19.196 --> 11:21.316 align:middle
We like to average over
three or six months,

11:21.396 --> 11:22.726 align:middle
but still that bears watching.

11:22.726 --> 11:23.986 align:middle
2 Chair Powell intended to say
"business fixed investment."

11:23.986 --> 11:25.806 align:middle
So we'll be monitoring
the implications of all

11:25.806 --> 11:27.766 align:middle
of those developments for
the U.S. economic outlook.

11:27.826 --> 11:29.916 align:middle
We expect to learn a good
deal more, as I mentioned.

11:29.916 --> 11:33.556 align:middle
And we'll be asking the question
whether those risks are going

11:33.556 --> 11:34.736 align:middle
to continue to weigh
on the outlook.

11:35.456 --> 11:38.786 align:middle
And then, in the end, we'll
use our tools as appropriate

11:38.856 --> 11:40.836 align:middle
to sustain this long expansion.

11:42.056 --> 11:42.346 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

11:42.776 --> 11:47.106 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

11:48.286 --> 11:51.406 align:middle
Could you clarify what you
would do if the President tweets

11:51.406 --> 11:55.446 align:middle
or calls you to say he would
like to demote you as Fed Chair?

11:56.086 --> 11:57.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

11:57.396 --> 12:01.296 align:middle
I think the law is clear
that I have a four-year term,

12:01.376 --> 12:04.006 align:middle
and I fully intend to serve it.

12:06.276 --> 12:08.466 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

12:09.926 --> 12:10.516 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

12:10.516 --> 12:13.166 align:middle
I was hoping that-this is Jeanna
Smialek from the New York Times.

12:13.166 --> 12:14.626 align:middle
I was hoping that
you could clarify

12:14.696 --> 12:18.036 align:middle
for us a little bit how you're
thinking about the risks

12:18.126 --> 12:21.376 align:middle
of waiting too long to
cut rates versus the risks

12:21.376 --> 12:23.816 align:middle
of cutting rates
prematurely-sort of,

12:23.816 --> 12:27.126 align:middle
what the balance of risks are
and how you talk about that.

12:27.696 --> 12:28.186 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

12:28.186 --> 12:30.156 align:middle
Right. So we're always
trying to balance that risk,

12:30.226 --> 12:34.306 align:middle
but I would say that, given
the quite recent nature of some

12:34.306 --> 12:38.586 align:middle
of the events, I think the
Committee felt, though,

12:38.586 --> 12:41.336 align:middle
that the right thing to do
was to wait and see more.

12:41.336 --> 12:43.126 align:middle
And we will see a lot more
on all of these issues

12:43.126 --> 12:44.046 align:middle
in the very near term.

12:44.046 --> 12:45.526 align:middle
So I don't think the risk

12:45.526 --> 12:47.826 align:middle
of waiting too long is
prominent right now.

12:47.826 --> 12:50.046 align:middle
I would say, as a general
matter, it's always something

12:50.046 --> 12:51.196 align:middle
that we have to weigh.

12:51.896 --> 12:56.276 align:middle
But I think we believe that
the right thing here is

12:56.386 --> 12:59.376 align:middle
to watch carefully
in the near term

12:59.376 --> 13:02.226 align:middle
and see how these risks unfold
and see whether they continue

13:02.226 --> 13:02.986 align:middle
to weigh on the outlook.

13:03.436 --> 13:05.356 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

13:06.106 --> 13:07.976 align:middle
[Inaudible] CHAIR POWELL.

13:10.036 --> 13:12.626 align:middle
Obviously, we try to avoid
going prematurely as well.

13:13.336 --> 13:16.936 align:middle
In this case, you know,
there's always some judgment

13:16.996 --> 13:18.136 align:middle
in these things.

13:19.036 --> 13:21.696 align:middle
But I would just
say that the risks

13:21.806 --> 13:27.236 align:middle
that we see having
emerged are risks

13:27.236 --> 13:30.546 align:middle
that have gotten our attention
and that have called a number

13:30.546 --> 13:32.456 align:middle
of us to write down rate
cuts, and a number of those

13:32.456 --> 13:38.316 align:middle
who haven't to see that
the case has strengthened.

13:40.696 --> 13:41.566 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

13:41.566 --> 13:43.106 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with the AP.

13:43.106 --> 13:46.706 align:middle
You had your first dissent
in your time as Chairman.

13:47.036 --> 13:52.746 align:middle
Does that give us a sense that
there was debate among a group

13:52.746 --> 13:55.236 align:middle
that was pushing for
a rate cut this time?

13:55.526 --> 14:00.426 align:middle
And how do you-do you expect
further dissents going forward?

14:01.526 --> 14:02.236 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:02.236 --> 14:04.826 align:middle
Let me let me say the same
thing as I said the last time,

14:04.826 --> 14:06.076 align:middle
before there had
been any dissents,

14:06.076 --> 14:09.736 align:middle
and that is that I think
the process of careful,

14:09.806 --> 14:12.526 align:middle
thoughtful dissent is
a very healthy one,

14:12.646 --> 14:14.456 align:middle
and I've always believed that.

14:14.456 --> 14:15.976 align:middle
And I feel like you
make better decisions

14:15.976 --> 14:17.246 align:middle
when you hear a disparity
of views.

14:17.246 --> 14:21.676 align:middle
So I really do look
at it that way.

14:22.216 --> 14:27.166 align:middle
I would add, though, that the
support for the path we took

14:27.166 --> 14:30.526 align:middle
for the policy statement that
we adopted was quite broad.

14:33.806 --> 14:37.476 align:middle
JAMES POLITI.

14:37.476 --> 14:40.026 align:middle
James Politi with
the Financial Times.

14:40.096 --> 14:43.756 align:middle
Mario Draghi at the ECB
yesterday sent a strong signal

14:44.126 --> 14:46.796 align:middle
of new stimulus for
the euro zone.

14:47.126 --> 14:50.706 align:middle
Do you think that such
actions to ease policy

14:50.706 --> 14:54.046 align:middle
at other central banks around
the world will put more pressure

14:54.046 --> 14:57.206 align:middle
on the Fed to do the same?

14:58.196 --> 14:58.736 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

14:58.876 --> 15:01.906 align:middle
Well, first, I think all
central banks are focused

15:01.906 --> 15:04.556 align:middle
on their domestic-their
mandates are domestic,

15:04.556 --> 15:07.056 align:middle
and they're focused
on economic conditions

15:07.806 --> 15:09.996 align:middle
from a domestic standpoint.

15:09.996 --> 15:11.676 align:middle
And that goes for the
European Central Bank,

15:11.676 --> 15:13.386 align:middle
it goes for the Fed, it
goes for all central banks.

15:13.386 --> 15:16.146 align:middle
So that's our principal focus.

15:16.596 --> 15:19.326 align:middle
So it could cut either way.

15:19.326 --> 15:21.326 align:middle
You know, I would think that,

15:21.456 --> 15:23.706 align:middle
to the extent you see
stronger financial conditions

15:23.706 --> 15:27.076 align:middle
and stronger activity in
the ECB after a rate cut,

15:27.146 --> 15:30.346 align:middle
that would support-tend
to support activity.3

15:30.346 --> 15:32.586 align:middle
So we're really focused
on, you know,

15:32.586 --> 15:35.936 align:middle
the risks to our-on
the baseline outlook,

15:36.036 --> 15:37.726 align:middle
which is still a
pretty favorable one,

15:37.726 --> 15:39.526 align:middle
and the risks to those outlooks.

15:39.566 --> 15:40.546 align:middle
That's our principal focus.

15:41.046 --> 15:42.906 align:middle
3 Chair Powell intended to say

15:43.376 --> 15:44.436 align:middle
"stronger activity
in the euro area."

15:44.496 --> 15:44.816 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

15:44.816 --> 15:45.746 align:middle
Thanks. This is the first time

15:45.746 --> 15:48.126 align:middle
that you've been really
issuing SEPs in an era

15:48.126 --> 15:49.626 align:middle
when rates are going
to be going down.

15:49.676 --> 15:52.526 align:middle
Just two sort of related
questions: Is there concern

15:52.996 --> 15:56.246 align:middle
that you'll be causing a
sort of "dot deflation"

15:56.246 --> 15:58.836 align:middle
by telling people, "Well,
don't buy your car now,

15:58.836 --> 16:00.546 align:middle
because it'll get
cheaper in six months

16:00.616 --> 16:03.106 align:middle
because we're cutting
rates," and that

16:03.106 --> 16:05.546 align:middle
that could sort of
fulfill itself?

16:05.546 --> 16:08.546 align:middle
And, secondly, on inflation,
that was a pretty big drop

16:08.546 --> 16:13.896 align:middle
in expected PCE, yet, you
know, without reacting to it,

16:14.376 --> 16:18.416 align:middle
are you not sort of undermining
your own credibility in terms

16:18.416 --> 16:20.246 align:middle
of commitment to the
2 percent target?

16:21.406 --> 16:21.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:22.436 --> 16:24.356 align:middle
I'll take the inflation
one first.

16:24.356 --> 16:25.936 align:middle
I didn't quite follow
your "dot" question.

16:26.166 --> 16:26.706 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

16:26.886 --> 16:29.756 align:middle
Well, the fact that
expected inflation went

16:29.756 --> 16:32.106 align:middle
from 1.8 to-Howard Schneider
with Reuters-went from 1.8

16:32.106 --> 16:34.916 align:middle
to 1.5, it's the fact that
you're not responding to that.

16:34.916 --> 16:35.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:35.126 --> 16:35.896 align:middle
That's the inflation question.

16:35.896 --> 16:36.206 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

16:36.206 --> 16:36.526 align:middle
Yes. CHAIR POWELL.

16:36.526 --> 16:37.356 align:middle
I'm saying you- HOWARD
SCHNEIDER.

16:37.356 --> 16:40.516 align:middle
On def-no, the fact that you've
signaled rate cuts are coming.

16:40.736 --> 16:42.646 align:middle
Or is-was there any
concern on the Committee

16:42.646 --> 16:46.506 align:middle
that this would tell consumers,
tell people, "Don't borrow now,

16:46.506 --> 16:48.726 align:middle
don't spend now, because
rates will get cheaper later"?

16:48.726 --> 16:49.856 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:49.856 --> 16:50.356 align:middle
I see. Okay, right.

16:50.356 --> 16:53.836 align:middle
Okay. So let me let me answer
the inflation question first.

16:53.836 --> 16:58.636 align:middle
So we're saying that we know-I
noted in the statement and also

16:58.636 --> 17:00.806 align:middle
in my-what I said here.

17:01.336 --> 17:03.456 align:middle
We saw that market-based
measures

17:03.456 --> 17:05.996 align:middle
of inflation expectations,
breakevens, dropped.

17:06.126 --> 17:07.856 align:middle
We noted that also
in the statement.

17:08.566 --> 17:12.056 align:middle
And I noted it as a
reason for us to-one

17:12.056 --> 17:15.426 align:middle
of several reasons why it
feels to us that the case

17:15.426 --> 17:17.796 align:middle
for more accommodation
has strengthened.

17:18.196 --> 17:20.096 align:middle
So we find that notable.

17:20.096 --> 17:24.666 align:middle
Not only that, the actual
forecast for inflation

17:24.666 --> 17:28.586 align:middle
for this year, among
FOMC participants,

17:28.876 --> 17:30.066 align:middle
dropped a couple of tenths.

17:30.066 --> 17:33.796 align:middle
So that means a more prolonged
shortfall of inflation.

17:33.906 --> 17:38.496 align:middle
Let me say, on inflation, it's
something I've been concerned

17:38.496 --> 17:40.136 align:middle
about for quite a long time.

17:40.136 --> 17:42.236 align:middle
It's one of the principal
reasons why I called

17:42.236 --> 17:44.256 align:middle
for the review.

17:44.316 --> 17:47.676 align:middle
In a world where policy
rates are going to be closer

17:47.676 --> 17:52.006 align:middle
to the effective lower bound,
then, just as a general matter,

17:52.006 --> 17:55.296 align:middle
we need to be really strong
on 2 percent inflation.

17:55.296 --> 17:59.906 align:middle
So I think, you know, we
certainly don't want to be seen

17:59.906 --> 18:02.226 align:middle
as weak on inflation, and
I don't believe we are.

18:02.526 --> 18:05.006 align:middle
In terms of the dots, you're
right, this is the first time,

18:05.006 --> 18:06.736 align:middle
I believe, we've
had-we've talked

18:06.736 --> 18:09.606 align:middle
about cutting in the "dot era."

18:09.606 --> 18:12.146 align:middle
I guess the dot era
began in January 2012.

18:12.826 --> 18:14.156 align:middle
And, you know, we're
working our way through it.

18:14.516 --> 18:17.946 align:middle
And I think it's
just something we do.

18:17.946 --> 18:20.216 align:middle
You know that my view on the
dots is that they, overall,

18:20.256 --> 18:24.786 align:middle
provide useful information
for people, but that we need

18:24.786 --> 18:28.546 align:middle
to do our absolute best
to explain what they are

18:28.546 --> 18:29.296 align:middle
and what they are not.

18:29.656 --> 18:32.036 align:middle
Speaking of which, they are
not a forecast of the group,

18:32.036 --> 18:35.006 align:middle
they're not discussed or debated
at the meeting, they're an input

18:35.006 --> 18:36.996 align:middle
to policy more than
an output of policy,

18:37.656 --> 18:39.946 align:middle
and they're also only
the most likely case.

18:39.946 --> 18:43.426 align:middle
So, in a situation where there's
relatively high uncertainty,

18:43.836 --> 18:45.046 align:middle
there's the most likely case,

18:45.126 --> 18:47.786 align:middle
but the second most likely
case might only be a little bit

18:47.786 --> 18:48.436 align:middle
less likely.

18:48.586 --> 18:49.876 align:middle
But that doesn't
show up in the dot.

18:49.876 --> 18:52.576 align:middle
The dot is either one
thing or it's another.

18:52.806 --> 18:56.836 align:middle
So I just would say that, if
you pay too close attention

18:56.836 --> 18:59.306 align:middle
to the dots, then you may lose
sight of the larger picture.

18:59.826 --> 19:01.616 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

19:03.786 --> 19:04.156 align:middle
Thank you.

19:04.156 --> 19:05.786 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

19:05.946 --> 19:10.716 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, if and when the
Committee decides to cut rates,

19:10.716 --> 19:13.186 align:middle
I suspect there'll be a
debate over whether to move

19:13.186 --> 19:15.206 align:middle
by 25 or 50 basis points.

19:15.566 --> 19:17.626 align:middle
Indeed, there's a
pretty substantial body

19:17.626 --> 19:21.516 align:middle
of academic literature arguing
that a central bank close

19:21.516 --> 19:26.266 align:middle
to the zero lower bound ought to
act sooner and more aggressively

19:26.436 --> 19:27.696 align:middle
than it otherwise would.

19:27.896 --> 19:30.006 align:middle
I'm wondering what you
think of that prescription,

19:30.006 --> 19:32.976 align:middle
and if you could spend a couple
of minutes discussing the pros

19:32.976 --> 19:35.886 align:middle
and cons of the 50
basis point cut

19:35.886 --> 19:37.246 align:middle
and how you approach
that question.

19:37.656 --> 19:38.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

19:38.046 --> 19:39.486 align:middle
On the specific question
of that,

19:39.536 --> 19:42.566 align:middle
that's just something we
haven't really engaged with yet,

19:42.626 --> 19:46.326 align:middle
and it will depend very
heavily on incoming data

19:46.326 --> 19:49.066 align:middle
and the evolving risk
picture as we move forward.

19:49.066 --> 19:52.306 align:middle
So it would be-so nothing I
can say about that is specific

19:52.346 --> 19:54.046 align:middle
to the near-term
question that we face.

19:54.516 --> 19:56.926 align:middle
More generally, though,
the research you refer

19:56.926 --> 20:01.756 align:middle
to essentially notes that, in
a world where you are closer

20:01.756 --> 20:05.526 align:middle
to the effective lower
bound, it's why-research kind

20:05.526 --> 20:10.516 align:middle
of shows this-it's wise
to react, for example,

20:10.566 --> 20:16.196 align:middle
to prevent a weakening
from turning

20:16.196 --> 20:17.236 align:middle
into a prolonged weakening.

20:17.396 --> 20:18.506 align:middle
In other words, sort of,

20:18.936 --> 20:20.966 align:middle
an ounce of prevention
is worth a pound of cure.

20:21.226 --> 20:23.466 align:middle
So I think that is
a valid way to think

20:23.466 --> 20:25.356 align:middle
about policy in this era.

20:25.356 --> 20:29.816 align:middle
I don't know-and it's always in
the-I think it's in the minds

20:29.816 --> 20:33.486 align:middle
of policymakers, you
know, during this era,

20:33.486 --> 20:36.516 align:middle
because it's well
understood to be correct.

20:36.786 --> 20:40.716 align:middle
Again, I don't know what that
means in terms of the size

20:40.716 --> 20:42.466 align:middle
of a particular rate
cut going forward.

20:42.466 --> 20:46.526 align:middle
That's going to depend heavily
upon, you know, the actual data

20:46.806 --> 20:47.876 align:middle
and the evolving risk picture.

20:48.416 --> 20:54.456 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

20:54.536 --> 20:55.786 align:middle
Donna Borak with CNN.

20:55.926 --> 20:56.976 align:middle
Thanks, Chairman Powell.

20:57.456 --> 21:00.446 align:middle
Democratic presidential
candidate Elizabeth Warren has

21:01.016 --> 21:04.786 align:middle
provided a proposal to revalue
the U.S. dollar in order

21:04.786 --> 21:06.886 align:middle
to address concerns about
rising trade deficits.

21:07.456 --> 21:09.806 align:middle
The President himself
has routinely complained

21:09.806 --> 21:11.206 align:middle
about the strength
of the U.S. dollar,

21:11.286 --> 21:13.476 align:middle
saying it has resulted
in, quote, "tremendous,"

21:13.986 --> 21:16.616 align:middle
close quote, competitive
advantage with countries

21:16.616 --> 21:17.556 align:middle
like China and others.

21:18.136 --> 21:20.966 align:middle
Do you think that an overvalued
dollar has been a drag

21:20.966 --> 21:23.196 align:middle
on America's global
competitiveness?

21:23.196 --> 21:24.966 align:middle
And would you support
an intervention

21:24.966 --> 21:26.446 align:middle
of some kind on this issue?

21:26.446 --> 21:27.446 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:27.986 --> 21:29.736 align:middle
The U.S. Treasury
has responsibility

21:29.826 --> 21:34.676 align:middle
for exchange rate policy, not
the Fed, and we don't comment,

21:35.056 --> 21:36.706 align:middle
in that sense, on the
level of the dollar.

21:36.926 --> 21:41.246 align:middle
We have the responsibility
for maximum employment

21:41.246 --> 21:43.836 align:middle
and stable prices, and we use
our tools to achieve that.

21:44.616 --> 21:47.636 align:middle
Of course, we do that through
changing financial conditions,

21:47.636 --> 21:49.886 align:middle
and one of those is the dollar,
but we don't target the dollar.

21:50.376 --> 21:51.406 align:middle
It's just something
that we don't do.

21:51.406 --> 21:56.066 align:middle
In fact, central
banks-or, rather, nations,

21:56.066 --> 21:59.016 align:middle
when they get together,
routinely adopt a communique

21:59.016 --> 22:01.016 align:middle
that says we will target
our domestic, economic,

22:01.246 --> 22:03.286 align:middle
and financial conditions
and not our exchange rate

22:03.286 --> 22:05.746 align:middle
in using monetary policy, and
that includes the United States,

22:06.066 --> 22:07.716 align:middle
that includes the
G-20 communique

22:07.716 --> 22:10.016 align:middle
that we adopted 10 days ago.

22:10.796 --> 22:14.996 align:middle
So I'm not the right person
to ask about that sort

22:14.996 --> 22:16.216 align:middle
of dollar policy intervention.

22:17.796 --> 22:18.506 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

22:21.856 --> 22:22.376 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

22:22.376 --> 22:25.636 align:middle
Paul Kiernan from Dow Jones.

22:25.946 --> 22:28.366 align:middle
If the most-according
to the dot plot,

22:28.366 --> 22:31.436 align:middle
I mean-if the most likely
case is that you will have

22:31.476 --> 22:35.046 align:middle
to cut rates in the next
18 months and given some

22:35.046 --> 22:39.226 align:middle
of the concerns about, you know,
policy needing to react sooner

22:39.226 --> 22:40.226 align:middle
and more aggressively,

22:40.716 --> 22:45.006 align:middle
what would have been the
downsides to cutting rates now?

22:45.006 --> 22:45.946 align:middle
Why not just cut them now?

22:46.896 --> 22:47.516 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

22:49.176 --> 22:51.556 align:middle
So, why not now?

22:52.096 --> 22:56.536 align:middle
And, I would say, there
was not much support

22:56.616 --> 22:58.706 align:middle
for cutting rates
now at this meeting.

22:58.776 --> 23:01.686 align:middle
There was, as you see, a number
of people wrote down rate cuts.

23:02.446 --> 23:04.996 align:middle
But all of those,
but apparently one,

23:05.266 --> 23:11.706 align:middle
felt that it would be better
to see more to-before moving.

23:11.706 --> 23:13.996 align:middle
And I gave a couple of
reasons why that is the case.

23:14.346 --> 23:17.486 align:middle
First, it's just the fact that
some of these developments are

23:17.486 --> 23:23.066 align:middle
so recent that we want to
see whether they'll sustain.

23:23.556 --> 23:25.726 align:middle
So we felt that it would be
better to get a clearer picture

23:25.726 --> 23:27.176 align:middle
of things, and that
we would, in fact,

23:27.176 --> 23:29.876 align:middle
learn a lot about these
developments in the near term.

23:30.556 --> 23:33.406 align:middle
Ultimately, the question we're
going to be asking ourselves is,

23:33.866 --> 23:35.796 align:middle
are these risks going
to be continuing

23:35.796 --> 23:37.846 align:middle
to weigh on the outlook?

23:38.366 --> 23:42.146 align:middle
And we will act as
needed, including promptly,

23:42.146 --> 23:44.956 align:middle
if that's appropriate,
and use our tools

23:44.956 --> 23:46.456 align:middle
to expand-to sustain
the expansion.

23:46.886 --> 23:48.556 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

23:49.026 --> 23:53.276 align:middle
Yes. Thank you for
doing this, Chairman.

23:53.276 --> 23:55.176 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business Network.

23:55.436 --> 23:58.066 align:middle
How do you reconcile the
conflicting economic data

23:58.066 --> 23:58.426 align:middle
coming in?

23:58.426 --> 24:01.016 align:middle
You know, on one hand, you
have strong overall growth.

24:01.206 --> 24:02.716 align:middle
Consumer spending is strong.

24:02.716 --> 24:05.346 align:middle
On the other hand, manufacturing
numbers were a little bit

24:05.346 --> 24:08.776 align:middle
weaker, you have growth in jobs
but coming in a little weaker,

24:08.896 --> 24:10.206 align:middle
and then you have low inflation.

24:10.626 --> 24:12.446 align:middle
And then, specifically,
what data are you looking

24:12.446 --> 24:15.426 align:middle
at that you decided
not to have a rate cut?

24:15.426 --> 24:16.566 align:middle
Why didn't you do that?

24:17.216 --> 24:17.716 align:middle
[0:24:15.5] CHAIR POWELL.

24:17.716 --> 24:19.456 align:middle
Well, you gave a
pretty good picture.

24:19.456 --> 24:22.346 align:middle
I mean, it's a complicated
picture, and, you know,

24:22.346 --> 24:23.846 align:middle
the answer is, we
look at all of it.

24:24.416 --> 24:28.216 align:middle
But I would say, the big
pieces of it are this:

24:28.796 --> 24:32.006 align:middle
The baseline outlook
has been a good one,

24:32.446 --> 24:35.566 align:middle
and that has basically been
consumer spending coming back

24:35.566 --> 24:36.516 align:middle
up in the second quarter.

24:36.936 --> 24:38.386 align:middle
That is coming true.

24:39.196 --> 24:41.456 align:middle
And consumer spending is

24:41.456 --> 24:44.446 align:middle
at a healthy level,
and that makes sense.

24:44.806 --> 24:46.096 align:middle
You've got a tight labor market.

24:46.406 --> 24:49.556 align:middle
You've got companies in surveys
saying that labor is scarce.

24:50.336 --> 24:53.326 align:middle
You've got workers in surveys
saying that jobs are plentiful.

24:53.556 --> 24:54.656 align:middle
You've got wages going up.

24:55.186 --> 24:56.736 align:middle
You've got high levels
of household confidence.

24:56.736 --> 24:59.006 align:middle
So, all of that,
underlying fundamentals

24:59.006 --> 25:01.166 align:middle
for the consumer spending
part of the economy,

25:01.166 --> 25:04.086 align:middle
which is 70 percent of the
economy, is quite solid.

25:04.626 --> 25:07.256 align:middle
Job creation, if you take
a three-month average,

25:07.256 --> 25:11.736 align:middle
is still well above, you
know, the entry-level

25:11.736 --> 25:12.856 align:middle
of entry into the workforce.

25:12.856 --> 25:14.996 align:middle
So that part of the
economy is solid.

25:15.446 --> 25:16.486 align:middle
You mentioned manufacturing,

25:16.646 --> 25:18.006 align:middle
and we're seeing this
all around the world.

25:18.146 --> 25:21.966 align:middle
Manufacturing, investment,
and trade have been weaker.

25:22.496 --> 25:26.466 align:middle
It's not solely a domestic
issue, and it may be

25:26.466 --> 25:29.836 align:middle
that there are a range of
factors that are contributing

25:29.996 --> 25:33.616 align:middle
to that, including, for
example, what China has done

25:33.616 --> 25:36.496 align:middle
over the last couple
of years in working

25:36.496 --> 25:37.796 align:middle
to bring down its leverage.

25:38.356 --> 25:44.096 align:middle
Some of it may be uncertainty
over your supply chains due

25:44.096 --> 25:46.256 align:middle
to trade developments.

25:46.626 --> 25:49.956 align:middle
The Boeing 737 issues may be
contributing in their own way.

25:49.956 --> 25:52.336 align:middle
So there-lower oil
prices are contributing

25:52.336 --> 25:56.286 align:middle
to lower investment,
although they're also leading

25:56.286 --> 25:58.136 align:middle
to lower gas prices,
which supports spending.

25:58.376 --> 25:59.356 align:middle
So there are many, many things.

25:59.356 --> 26:01.476 align:middle
There isn't any one thing
that explains it all.

26:01.476 --> 26:04.656 align:middle
But it's something
that we're watching.

26:05.206 --> 26:09.416 align:middle
But you do see growth in
services, so you-this pattern

26:09.416 --> 26:12.156 align:middle
around the world of weak
manufacturing but growth

26:12.156 --> 26:16.326 align:middle
in the far larger part of the
services economy, which has led

26:16.326 --> 26:19.156 align:middle
to low unemployment, good
job creation, rising wages,

26:19.156 --> 26:22.536 align:middle
that's kind of the two big
pieces of it that you see.

26:23.216 --> 26:25.086 align:middle
Then you see the crosscurrents.

26:25.086 --> 26:28.166 align:middle
If you lay the crosscurrents
on top of that-concerns

26:28.256 --> 26:31.056 align:middle
about global growth and trade
developments-you have the

26:31.056 --> 26:31.716 align:middle
full picture.

26:31.926 --> 26:34.346 align:middle
And I think what that
picture-what we took away

26:34.346 --> 26:37.096 align:middle
from that picture is that
we'd like to see more,

26:37.886 --> 26:41.836 align:middle
that we do see these risks,
and what we want to do is,

26:41.836 --> 26:43.936 align:middle
we want to watch and see
whether they continue

26:43.936 --> 26:44.956 align:middle
to weigh upon the outlook.

26:46.586 --> 26:48.056 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

26:49.946 --> 26:51.746 align:middle
Michael McKee, Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

26:52.396 --> 26:54.496 align:middle
If consumer spending is solid

26:54.496 --> 26:57.766 align:middle
and business investment has
been slowed by uncertainty,

26:58.226 --> 27:01.206 align:middle
I'd like to get your thinking
on what a Fed rate cut would do?

27:01.726 --> 27:04.866 align:middle
Have you modeled the
additional growth

27:04.866 --> 27:07.036 align:middle
and inflation you might
get from a rate cut?

27:07.086 --> 27:10.386 align:middle
Can you identify any
sectors that would benefit

27:10.386 --> 27:12.196 align:middle
from a lower cost of capital?

27:12.446 --> 27:15.316 align:middle
Or is this really about the Fed
being the only game in town?

27:16.446 --> 27:17.076 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

27:17.076 --> 27:20.096 align:middle
Well, we have the tools we have,
and we're committed and sworn

27:20.096 --> 27:22.026 align:middle
to use them to support
economic activity.

27:22.026 --> 27:25.736 align:middle
And they do support economic
activity through a number

27:25.736 --> 27:29.666 align:middle
of channels that are reasonably
well understood-some more

27:29.666 --> 27:31.376 align:middle
directly tied to interest
rates than others.

27:31.456 --> 27:35.226 align:middle
But we do generally believe
that-that our interest rate

27:35.226 --> 27:38.316 align:middle
policy can support demand

27:38.866 --> 27:40.866 align:middle
and support business
investment as well.

27:41.126 --> 27:46.456 align:middle
And so we will use those
tools and use them as we see

27:46.456 --> 27:48.976 align:middle
as appropriate to achieve our
objectives, which really are

27:49.036 --> 27:53.806 align:middle
to sustain this expansion, and I
would just make a note of that.

27:54.506 --> 27:56.416 align:middle
The reason why we say
"sustain the expansion" is,

27:56.536 --> 27:59.556 align:middle
you're seeing now, for
the first time, you know,

28:00.356 --> 28:03.876 align:middle
communities that are being
brought into the benefits

28:03.876 --> 28:06.556 align:middle
of this expansion that
hadn't been earlier.

28:06.626 --> 28:08.126 align:middle
You're 10 years deep into this,

28:08.126 --> 28:11.356 align:middle
and that's something we heard
quite a lot at the conference

28:11.356 --> 28:12.476 align:middle
in Chicago on the review.

28:13.146 --> 28:15.376 align:middle
And I just would say,
that's why we think-it's one

28:15.376 --> 28:16.926 align:middle
of the reasons why we
think it's so important

28:16.926 --> 28:19.536 align:middle
to sustain the expansion,
keep it going,

28:20.016 --> 28:23.376 align:middle
because we really are benefiting
groups that haven't seen,

28:23.876 --> 28:25.896 align:middle
you know, this kind of
prosperity in a long time.

28:26.026 --> 28:26.356 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

28:26.356 --> 28:27.816 align:middle
But given your description
of the crosscurrents,

28:28.346 --> 28:30.666 align:middle
do you think Fed policy
can solve those problems?

28:31.346 --> 28:31.726 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

28:32.396 --> 28:35.966 align:middle
So we take the-you know, we take
the crosscurrents as a given,

28:36.166 --> 28:37.976 align:middle
and we have our tools.

28:38.736 --> 28:45.536 align:middle
You know, we don't-we react
to anything in principle

28:46.216 --> 28:48.736 align:middle
that could undermine
our achievement

28:48.736 --> 28:50.976 align:middle
of our dual-mandate
goals-maximum employment,

28:50.976 --> 28:53.746 align:middle
stable prices-is
worthy of our attention

28:53.836 --> 28:55.756 align:middle
and can call forth
a policy response.

28:56.756 --> 29:02.656 align:middle
And that's just how
we look at it.

29:04.386 --> 29:07.566 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

29:07.696 --> 29:10.256 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

29:10.616 --> 29:12.776 align:middle
You've said that the Fed doesn't
take short-term political

29:12.776 --> 29:14.256 align:middle
considerations into account,

29:14.256 --> 29:15.976 align:middle
and you've defended
the Fed's independence.

29:16.626 --> 29:20.406 align:middle
So I was wondering, is there a
point at which you think that,

29:20.406 --> 29:22.686 align:middle
publicly or privately,
you should push back

29:22.726 --> 29:25.826 align:middle
on the President's criticisms
rather than ignoring him?

29:26.286 --> 29:28.826 align:middle
And also, do you think that you

29:28.826 --> 29:30.246 align:middle
and the President
have the same goals

29:30.246 --> 29:31.636 align:middle
when it comes to
monetary policy?

29:32.196 --> 29:33.396 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

29:33.396 --> 29:36.046 align:middle
You know, I don't-I don't
discuss elected officials

29:36.926 --> 29:38.316 align:middle
publicly or privately, really.

29:38.416 --> 29:41.516 align:middle
So I would just say
that we are-at the Fed,

29:41.516 --> 29:47.246 align:middle
we're deeply committed to
carrying out our mission,

29:47.246 --> 29:49.106 align:middle
and also that our independence

29:49.186 --> 29:51.126 align:middle
from direct political
control we see

29:51.126 --> 29:53.136 align:middle
as an important institutional
feature

29:53.136 --> 29:55.536 align:middle
that has served both the
economy and the country well.

29:56.036 --> 29:59.266 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

29:59.806 --> 30:02.316 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

30:02.876 --> 30:06.256 align:middle
Chair Powell, are you concerned
that new digital currencies

30:06.256 --> 30:09.236 align:middle
like Libra, which Facebook
unveiled this week,

30:09.756 --> 30:14.126 align:middle
could undermine the Fed
and erode your power

30:14.126 --> 30:15.536 align:middle
to influence the economy?

30:15.836 --> 30:19.286 align:middle
And did anyone from
Facebook talk with anyone

30:19.286 --> 30:22.316 align:middle
at the Fed before Libra
was unveiled this week?

30:22.846 --> 30:24.046 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

30:24.046 --> 30:26.066 align:middle
So, on your specific question

30:26.146 --> 30:30.166 align:middle
of digital currencies replacing
central bank currencies,

30:31.246 --> 30:33.906 align:middle
I think we're a long
way from that.

30:34.256 --> 30:37.066 align:middle
And, of course, the-so I think
we're a long way from that.

30:37.066 --> 30:39.766 align:middle
Digital currencies
are in their infancy.

30:39.766 --> 30:44.826 align:middle
So, essentially, not too
concerned about, you know,

30:44.826 --> 30:46.886 align:middle
the central banks no
longer being able to carry

30:46.886 --> 30:48.866 align:middle
out monetary policy
because of cryptocurrencies

30:48.866 --> 30:50.976 align:middle
or digital currencies.

30:51.506 --> 30:53.396 align:middle
You know, Facebook, I believe,

30:53.396 --> 30:58.366 align:middle
has made quite broad rounds
in-around the world really

30:58.366 --> 31:01.986 align:middle
with regulators,
supervisors, and lots of people

31:02.066 --> 31:04.906 align:middle
to discuss their plans, and
that certainly includes us.

31:05.766 --> 31:08.186 align:middle
And we're-you know, it's
something we're looking at.

31:08.396 --> 31:10.376 align:middle
We meet with a broad range

31:10.376 --> 31:13.506 align:middle
of private-sector firms all the
time on financial technology,

31:14.236 --> 31:16.176 align:middle
and there's just a
tremendous amount

31:16.176 --> 31:17.656 align:middle
of innovation going
on out there.

31:17.976 --> 31:20.626 align:middle
You know, there are
potential benefits here.

31:20.626 --> 31:24.546 align:middle
There are also potential risks,
particularly of a currency

31:24.546 --> 31:27.806 align:middle
that could, you know, that
could potentially have

31:27.806 --> 31:28.696 align:middle
large application.

31:29.416 --> 31:33.766 align:middle
So I would echo what
Governor Carney said,

31:33.766 --> 31:37.796 align:middle
which is that we will wind up
having quite high expectations,

31:38.096 --> 31:40.016 align:middle
from a sort of safety
and soundness

31:40.016 --> 31:42.486 align:middle
and regulatory standpoint,
if they do decide

31:42.486 --> 31:43.346 align:middle
to go forward with something.

31:43.536 --> 31:44.116 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

31:44.116 --> 31:45.426 align:middle
Do you think the
Fed will be involved

31:45.486 --> 31:47.206 align:middle
in regulating Libra then?

31:47.206 --> 31:47.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

31:47.926 --> 31:50.246 align:middle
You know, we have-we don't
have plenary authority

31:50.246 --> 31:52.086 align:middle
over cryptocurrencies as such.

31:52.206 --> 31:56.396 align:middle
They play into our world
through consumer protection

31:56.396 --> 31:58.716 align:middle
and money laundering
and things like that.

31:59.256 --> 32:02.376 align:middle
But, I would say that, you know,
through international forums,

32:03.306 --> 32:06.806 align:middle
you know, we have
significant input

32:06.806 --> 32:11.776 align:middle
into the payment system and, as
you know, play an important role

32:11.776 --> 32:13.856 align:middle
in the payment system
here in the United States.

32:15.056 --> 32:15.986 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

32:20.176 --> 32:21.046 align:middle
Thank you, Chairman Powell.

32:21.046 --> 32:22.466 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

32:23.016 --> 32:26.676 align:middle
I want to take you back
to Chicago and the review

32:27.616 --> 32:29.616 align:middle
of your monetary
policy strategy.

32:30.536 --> 32:32.896 align:middle
And something interesting,
I think, is developing,

32:32.896 --> 32:37.936 align:middle
is that outside experts are
excited and are-like the idea

32:37.936 --> 32:39.906 align:middle
of the Fed shifting
your inflation target

32:39.906 --> 32:42.946 align:middle
up to 4 percent-around
there, roughly, I guess.

32:43.296 --> 32:47.926 align:middle
They think that that would,
you know, help monetary policy,

32:47.926 --> 32:50.826 align:middle
and that there's no-2 percent
is not, like, sanctimonious

32:50.826 --> 32:52.316 align:middle
or anything, or whatever,
sacred.

32:53.286 --> 32:55.736 align:middle
It seems that you've
taken that off the table.

32:55.736 --> 32:57.966 align:middle
So I was wondering if
you could discuss that.

32:57.966 --> 32:59.446 align:middle
Have you taken it off the table?

32:59.446 --> 33:02.446 align:middle
And then, if so, why, or-and
what's your thoughts on that?

33:03.016 --> 33:06.146 align:middle
Thank you.

33:06.146 --> 33:06.586 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

33:06.586 --> 33:08.716 align:middle
We have-we've said
that we wouldn't look

33:08.776 --> 33:15.066 align:middle
at raising the target
rate for inflation.

33:15.216 --> 33:15.936 align:middle
We did say that.

33:16.126 --> 33:21.156 align:middle
And the reason is, it's become
a global norm, 2 percent.

33:21.776 --> 33:26.406 align:middle
Our statutory mandate
is price stability.

33:27.176 --> 33:30.416 align:middle
And so we're actually
taking a less-we're looking

33:30.416 --> 33:32.876 align:middle
at less radical ideas,
such as how

33:32.876 --> 33:35.766 align:middle
to make the 2 percent inflation
objective more credible.

33:36.656 --> 33:39.226 align:middle
This gives me a chance to say a
couple things about the review

33:39.226 --> 33:40.576 align:middle
and Chicago, in particular.

33:40.576 --> 33:41.796 align:middle
So it's a new thing for us.

33:41.796 --> 33:43.986 align:middle
It's something that-I thought
it was both appropriate

33:43.986 --> 33:44.996 align:middle
and important for us to do.

33:45.786 --> 33:49.506 align:middle
It'll be a yearlong, or
even longer, process,

33:49.506 --> 33:51.566 align:middle
looking at our strategy,
tools, and communications.

33:52.116 --> 33:54.656 align:middle
And it's meant to be a
way to open ourselves up,

33:54.716 --> 33:58.416 align:middle
to let the sunshine in, and
have dialogue and criticism

33:58.546 --> 34:00.256 align:middle
with the constituencies
that we serve.

34:00.316 --> 34:03.026 align:middle
We've had a series of Fed
Listens around the country

34:03.026 --> 34:03.996 align:middle
at every Reserve Bank,

34:04.136 --> 34:06.956 align:middle
and we had an academic
conference earlier this month,

34:07.076 --> 34:09.686 align:middle
with seven papers
written and criticized,

34:09.686 --> 34:10.846 align:middle
and leading global experts.

34:10.926 --> 34:13.386 align:middle
But I'll just say,
again, that the heart

34:13.386 --> 34:17.156 align:middle
of the conference was the two
panels on-with practitioners

34:17.156 --> 34:19.076 align:middle
in low and moderate
income-who live in low-

34:19.076 --> 34:20.746 align:middle
and moderate-income communities

34:21.896 --> 34:23.546 align:middle
and are part of those
communities.

34:23.636 --> 34:26.476 align:middle
And they were-there, I think,
people were quite struck

34:26.566 --> 34:30.106 align:middle
by their intervention,
which was really uniformly

34:30.106 --> 34:32.676 align:middle
around how important
maximum employment is

34:32.676 --> 34:34.146 align:middle
and what it means in
their communities.

34:34.146 --> 34:37.026 align:middle
You know, the idea being,
they haven't had, you know,

34:37.026 --> 34:38.716 align:middle
a bull market in
these communities.

34:38.716 --> 34:41.256 align:middle
They haven't had, just,
a booming economy.

34:41.256 --> 34:43.476 align:middle
What they have had
is low unemployment,

34:43.476 --> 34:44.826 align:middle
lots of social problems.

34:45.336 --> 34:49.646 align:middle
And just now, you have, you
know, companies who want to hire

34:50.236 --> 34:53.796 align:middle
and are bringing people into
the-providing opportunities

34:53.796 --> 34:55.176 align:middle
for people to come
into the labor force

34:55.536 --> 34:57.286 align:middle
to an extent not seen
in quite a long time.

34:58.196 --> 35:02.736 align:middle
And that is, I think, you know,
for someone who does this work,

35:02.736 --> 35:06.796 align:middle
that was very focusing
and motivating, too.

35:06.916 --> 35:08.396 align:middle
So I think everybody
thought, you know,

35:08.396 --> 35:10.066 align:middle
that's-that was really
quite worth doing.

35:10.066 --> 35:11.536 align:middle
I mean, there was some
thought at the beginning

35:11.536 --> 35:13.666 align:middle
that we should-some
people recommended

35:13.666 --> 35:16.746 align:middle
that we just talk to, you know,
econ Ph.D.'s about this, but,

35:16.896 --> 35:18.406 align:middle
no, that's not what
we chose to do,

35:18.406 --> 35:20.206 align:middle
and we're glad this
is the choice we made.

35:21.096 --> 35:22.846 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

35:24.566 --> 35:26.856 align:middle
But even those two panels
that you just referenced,

35:26.936 --> 35:29.966 align:middle
the people that spoke, they-when
they were asked about, you know,

35:29.966 --> 35:31.726 align:middle
2 percent inflation
or higher inflation,

35:31.726 --> 35:33.236 align:middle
they kind of shrugged
their shoulders, you know?

35:33.346 --> 35:36.756 align:middle
So-is 4 percent inflation
radical?

35:36.876 --> 35:38.036 align:middle
And to who?

35:38.646 --> 35:39.096 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

35:39.096 --> 35:42.806 align:middle
I don't think it's-I don't think
it's a practical alternative.

35:43.336 --> 35:44.536 align:middle
And I'll tell you why.

35:44.636 --> 35:50.006 align:middle
I think you see disinflationary
pressures around the world.

35:50.676 --> 35:53.556 align:middle
You see central banks having
a hard time getting inflation

35:53.556 --> 35:55.496 align:middle
up to their-close
to their objective.

35:55.686 --> 35:59.566 align:middle
We've done better than
other large central banks

35:59.956 --> 36:02.666 align:middle
that are not, you know, open
economies, like the U.K.,

36:02.666 --> 36:05.826 align:middle
where you have big
currency moves

36:05.826 --> 36:06.826 align:middle
that move inflation around.

36:07.316 --> 36:11.536 align:middle
So-but it's quite challenging
to get inflation-it's been-even

36:11.536 --> 36:13.406 align:middle
with very high levels
of resource utilization,

36:13.406 --> 36:18.426 align:middle
inflation has been lingering and
not getting back up to target

36:18.426 --> 36:20.426 align:middle
in a sustained, symmetric
kind of a way.

36:20.426 --> 36:21.886 align:middle
So saying that you're
going to go

36:21.886 --> 36:27.846 align:middle
for 4 percent-I wonder
how credible that will be.

36:28.106 --> 36:28.766 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

36:33.216 --> 36:34.726 align:middle
John Heltman with
American Banker.

36:34.756 --> 36:38.236 align:middle
I have a question about
the Fed's-or, I guess,

36:38.446 --> 36:43.326 align:middle
the bank regulators' leveraged
lending guidance from 2013.

36:43.326 --> 36:46.166 align:middle
As you know, the GAO
had a letter saying

36:46.166 --> 36:50.686 align:middle
that they thought it was a
rule, and it sort of went away.

36:51.296 --> 36:55.116 align:middle
But yet leveraged lending
is a source of concern.

36:55.296 --> 36:56.166 align:middle
You yourself have said

36:56.556 --> 36:59.756 align:middle
that credit underwriting
quality seems

36:59.756 --> 37:01.296 align:middle
to be deteriorating
somewhat lately,

37:01.756 --> 37:06.416 align:middle
and that the Fed has
tools to supervise banks

37:06.486 --> 37:09.286 align:middle
and to prevent leveraged lending

37:09.286 --> 37:10.906 align:middle
from becoming too
much of an issue.

37:11.086 --> 37:15.936 align:middle
I want to know what the-is
the 2013 guidance still

37:16.326 --> 37:19.236 align:middle
representative of the,
sort of, Fed's thinking

37:19.276 --> 37:21.186 align:middle
about leveraged lending?

37:21.766 --> 37:25.866 align:middle
And does the Fed have
any intent-intention

37:25.866 --> 37:29.146 align:middle
to either issue, like,
a leveraged lending rule

37:29.146 --> 37:32.686 align:middle
or a new guidance that
is less problematic?

37:33.146 --> 37:35.306 align:middle
Or is the plan to
just kind of carry

37:35.906 --> 37:40.006 align:middle
on with supervision
as you're-as you are?

37:41.026 --> 37:41.426 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

37:41.426 --> 37:46.836 align:middle
So the 2013 guidance is not
binding, and that's what came

37:46.836 --> 37:47.876 align:middle
out of the GAO review.

37:48.606 --> 37:51.836 align:middle
But that's really the
beginning of this-of the story.

37:51.836 --> 37:55.376 align:middle
You know, we have the authority
we need to examine the banks

37:55.376 --> 37:57.046 align:middle
for safety and soundness
exposure.

37:57.046 --> 38:00.156 align:middle
So, this-the first
thing you start

38:00.156 --> 38:03.816 align:middle
with as a bank supervisor is the
risks that the banks are taking

38:03.816 --> 38:06.546 align:middle
to themselves through their
portfolio, through the risks

38:06.546 --> 38:10.056 align:middle
that they're running and
the pipeline-you know,

38:10.056 --> 38:13.066 align:middle
the obligations that they've
undertaken to underwrite deals.

38:13.576 --> 38:17.586 align:middle
And so we monitor that very
carefully-so do the banks-and

38:17.586 --> 38:20.606 align:middle
you see exposures
that are much smaller

38:20.606 --> 38:22.086 align:middle
than they were before
the crisis.

38:22.086 --> 38:25.706 align:middle
And, by the way, we test that
regularly in the stress tests.

38:26.166 --> 38:28.616 align:middle
We impose very large
losses on those portfolios,

38:28.616 --> 38:31.086 align:middle
so we kind of have a
sense of what that is,

38:31.086 --> 38:35.916 align:middle
whereas before the crisis, there
was a lot of lack of knowledge

38:35.916 --> 38:37.636 align:middle
about what the losses would be.

38:38.166 --> 38:41.196 align:middle
So that's where it starts for
us in supervision, is the risks

38:41.196 --> 38:44.896 align:middle
that the banks are running-you
know, running on their own books

38:44.926 --> 38:46.766 align:middle
and to themselves
and to each other.

38:46.766 --> 38:50.206 align:middle
So, in that, I feel-I feel like
that is-that's in a good place,

38:50.256 --> 38:52.636 align:middle
but we never-we never say
"Mission accomplished" on that.

38:52.636 --> 38:55.086 align:middle
We will keep-you know, keep
monitoring that carefully.

38:55.716 --> 39:00.246 align:middle
What's happened, though,
is, the paper is now owned

39:00.446 --> 39:04.616 align:middle
by market-based vehicles:
collateralized loan obligations,

39:05.156 --> 39:06.566 align:middle
mutual funds, and
things like that.

39:06.566 --> 39:09.446 align:middle
And so we now have-you know,
we have a good sense of,

39:09.446 --> 39:11.296 align:middle
domestically, of
where that paper is.

39:11.616 --> 39:14.206 align:middle
I think, internationally,
not as much,

39:14.206 --> 39:17.726 align:middle
and the Financial Stability
Board is actually looking more

39:17.756 --> 39:19.266 align:middle
carefully at that.

39:19.266 --> 39:21.756 align:middle
And we-you know, we
monitor those vehicles

39:21.836 --> 39:23.396 align:middle
to see what they are.

39:23.396 --> 39:25.496 align:middle
And they're actually pretty
stably funded, in the sense

39:25.496 --> 39:26.566 align:middle
that there's no run risk,

39:27.256 --> 39:29.086 align:middle
but there's still
macroeconomic risk.

39:29.506 --> 39:32.236 align:middle
And, you know, this is something
that we take very seriously

39:32.236 --> 39:36.556 align:middle
and that the FSOC, the Financial
Stability Oversight Council,

39:36.556 --> 39:37.256 align:middle
is looking at.

39:37.886 --> 39:40.956 align:middle
And, you know, we call it
out as a macroeconomic risk,

39:41.146 --> 39:43.626 align:middle
but it's not really a financial
stability risk, in the sense

39:43.686 --> 39:48.326 align:middle
that it could undermine the
ability of the financial system

39:48.326 --> 39:50.036 align:middle
to do its job of
intermediating credit.

39:52.836 --> 39:55.716 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

39:55.836 --> 39:59.176 align:middle
Is there any intention, is there
any plan on the part of the Fed

39:59.176 --> 40:00.586 align:middle
or other regulators to sort

40:00.586 --> 40:04.646 align:middle
of create any additional
clarity for, or-and consistency,

40:04.646 --> 40:06.216 align:middle
really-that's the other
point of guidance,

40:06.216 --> 40:09.006 align:middle
right?-is to make sure
everyone knows kind

40:09.006 --> 40:11.786 align:middle
of what the supervisory
expectations are

40:11.786 --> 40:14.706 align:middle
for leveraged lending
and for all-for anything,

40:15.006 --> 40:15.586 align:middle
for that matter.

40:15.586 --> 40:17.536 align:middle
Is anything else coming?

40:17.636 --> 40:20.656 align:middle
Or is it just going to remain
kind of, like, bilateral kind

40:20.656 --> 40:21.816 align:middle
of conversations with banks?

40:22.826 --> 40:23.066 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

40:23.066 --> 40:26.086 align:middle
You know, I think
the issue isn't

40:26.416 --> 40:28.226 align:middle
that the banks don't
understand what the rules are.

40:28.226 --> 40:30.136 align:middle
The issue is that the
risk isn't in the banks.

40:30.826 --> 40:32.726 align:middle
It's in-it's out in those
market-based vehicles.

40:32.726 --> 40:36.966 align:middle
So I don't, I don't-you know, I
no longer am day-to-day involved

40:36.966 --> 40:40.246 align:middle
in this as I was
before I took this job.

40:40.576 --> 40:43.596 align:middle
But my sense is, though, that
that's really not the problem.

40:43.596 --> 40:44.646 align:middle
I'm not saying it's perfect,

40:44.646 --> 40:47.806 align:middle
but I think we do understand
what risks the banks

40:47.806 --> 40:48.166 align:middle
are running.

40:48.516 --> 40:51.506 align:middle
And really the question is,
how concerned should we be

40:51.506 --> 40:54.426 align:middle
about large holdings by
market-based vehicles

40:54.426 --> 40:57.186 align:middle
that I mentioned, and what
risks do they present?

40:57.186 --> 40:59.036 align:middle
And we're very carefully
assessing that.

40:59.176 --> 41:01.956 align:middle
And we continue to take
all these risks seriously.

41:01.956 --> 41:04.636 align:middle
You know, I gave a whole speech
about this a few weeks ago, so.

41:06.106 --> 41:07.126 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

41:08.626 --> 41:10.706 align:middle
Hi. Brian Cheung
with Yahoo Finance.

41:10.706 --> 41:13.356 align:middle
I'm just wondering if you
could expand a little bit

41:13.356 --> 41:14.276 align:middle
on the labor market.

41:14.326 --> 41:17.536 align:middle
So the statement noted that
the labor market still remains

41:17.536 --> 41:20.316 align:middle
strong, but the May jobs
report that that we saw missed

41:20.316 --> 41:22.456 align:middle
on estimates, but
employment rates still stayed

41:22.456 --> 41:23.656 align:middle
at 3.6 percent.

41:24.086 --> 41:25.826 align:middle
I'm wondering how you
reconcile that with the fact

41:25.896 --> 41:28.436 align:middle
that we only got 3.1 percent
year-over-year wage growth

41:28.436 --> 41:29.176 align:middle
in that report.

41:29.506 --> 41:31.206 align:middle
What does that tell
you about employment,

41:31.206 --> 41:33.456 align:middle
and by all standards
compared to, say, a month ago

41:33.456 --> 41:35.596 align:middle
or a year ago, are we
closer or farther away

41:35.596 --> 41:37.736 align:middle
from full employment
or maximum employment?

41:37.996 --> 41:38.716 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:38.856 --> 41:39.876 align:middle
You know, we have to be closer,

41:39.876 --> 41:42.046 align:middle
because more jobs
are being created

41:42.046 --> 41:43.536 align:middle
than people are entering
the labor force.

41:43.606 --> 41:45.676 align:middle
The unemployment rate is lower.

41:47.316 --> 41:50.286 align:middle
You know, by just lots
and lots of numbers,

41:50.836 --> 41:52.986 align:middle
the labor market
is in a good place.

41:53.256 --> 41:54.146 align:middle
You mentioned wages.

41:54.146 --> 41:58.116 align:middle
So the level of wages
is very consistent

41:58.166 --> 42:01.646 align:middle
with what it should
be, in the sense

42:01.736 --> 42:03.376 align:middle
that it's approximately equal

42:03.376 --> 42:07.586 align:middle
to inflation plus productivity
increases on an hourly basis.

42:07.996 --> 42:10.836 align:middle
So what's, I guess, a
little surprising, though,

42:10.836 --> 42:12.296 align:middle
is that you could
reach these levels

42:12.296 --> 42:16.136 align:middle
of unemployment late-you know,
long into a cycle, let's say,

42:16.536 --> 42:18.536 align:middle
and not see even higher
wages that are pushing

42:18.536 --> 42:20.876 align:middle
up on inflation, because
wages at this level,

42:20.876 --> 42:22.526 align:middle
even though they're
growing at a healthy rate,

42:22.936 --> 42:25.606 align:middle
at an appropriate rate,
they're not growing at a rate

42:25.606 --> 42:28.336 align:middle
that would provide much
upward thrust for inflation.

42:29.146 --> 42:33.116 align:middle
So, you know, we watch-we watch
all of this very carefully,

42:33.176 --> 42:39.276 align:middle
and I think we're very careful
about not assuming that we're,

42:39.536 --> 42:41.096 align:middle
you know, that would-that
there's no more slack

42:41.096 --> 42:41.806 align:middle
in the labor market.

42:42.396 --> 42:44.686 align:middle
You know, we've all
lived through-you know,

42:45.046 --> 42:48.596 align:middle
when I got to the Fed, we were
in the 8 percent-plus range,

42:48.596 --> 42:50.306 align:middle
and it's just gone
down and down and down,

42:50.386 --> 42:52.336 align:middle
and you haven't seen
wages picked up.

42:52.336 --> 42:56.936 align:middle
You haven't seen real
signals that we're

42:56.936 --> 42:58.246 align:middle
at maximum employment.

42:58.246 --> 42:59.746 align:middle
You have seen a tightening
labor market.

43:00.466 --> 43:03.986 align:middle
You know, it-the surveys that
I mentioned will all show

43:03.986 --> 43:06.256 align:middle
that the labor market
has tightened,

43:06.466 --> 43:07.486 align:middle
but not overtightened.

43:10.156 --> 43:10.636 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

43:12.416 --> 43:13.996 align:middle
Hi, Jean Yung with Market News.

43:14.206 --> 43:17.796 align:middle
I wanted to ask-did the
FOMC discuss a change

43:17.836 --> 43:19.966 align:middle
to its balance sheet
policy at this meeting,

43:20.336 --> 43:23.256 align:middle
perhaps ending runoffs
earlier than planned?

43:23.256 --> 43:26.356 align:middle
And would the Committee be
inclined to do something

43:26.356 --> 43:28.586 align:middle
like that if it lowers
rates before September?

43:28.586 --> 43:29.266 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:29.396 --> 43:32.596 align:middle
So, of course, we haven't
made any decisions yet.

43:33.696 --> 43:35.846 align:middle
Balance sheet runoff
is very close

43:35.846 --> 43:38.926 align:middle
to the end of its planned life.

43:39.456 --> 43:41.736 align:middle
I would say this: If
we do provide more

43:41.736 --> 43:44.636 align:middle
accommodation-again, we haven't
really addressed this-but

43:44.636 --> 43:46.126 align:middle
if we do provide
more accommodation,

43:46.636 --> 43:49.406 align:middle
we'll certainly keep in mind
what we said earlier this year,

43:49.406 --> 43:51.506 align:middle
which is that we'll
always be willing

43:51.506 --> 43:52.906 align:middle
to adjust balance sheet policy

43:52.906 --> 43:55.266 align:middle
so that it serves our
dual-mandate objectives.

43:56.736 --> 44:04.456 align:middle
Thank you very much.

